use std
into drop.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with dr_ is exported). It runs an explicit drop test on a voxel finite element model: the part starts touching a rigid frictionless floor while moving toward it, and central-difference time steps (lumped masses, half-step velocities) carry it through the impact and the rebound. The floor pushes back with a penalty spring on every node that passes it. It tracks the contact force, the center of mass velocity, the energies, and the worst von Mises stress and deformation each place reaches. Its only import line is `import math;`, ending with a semicolon. Every int used in real math is converted with `as real`, and a real used as an int with `as int`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls.

```amni
extern fn dr_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "dr_view_f64";
extern fn dr_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "dr_view_i32";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NN` (grid nodes), `GX`, `GY` and `GZ` (elements along x, y and z), `NH` (history records it may hold), `STEP` (steps taken), `NREC` (records written), `STRIDE` (steps between records, at least 1) and `DONE` (1 when finished). Reals starting at 0.0: `T` (time), `DT` (time step), `PF` (floor position along the drop direction), `MT` (total mass), `E0` (starting energy), `QUIET` (time the floor force has been 0 since contact ended) and `TOUCH` (1.0 once the floor has pushed). Growable f64 arrays: `IN` (64 inputs), `OUT` (64 outputs), `KE` (576: the 24 by 24 element stiffness, row by row), `DB` (144: the 6 by 24 matrix, row by row, that turns the 24 node displacements of one element into its 6 center stresses), `M` (NN lumped node masses, 0.0 for nodes no element uses), `X` (3 NN node coordinates), `U` (3 NN displacements), `V` (3 NN velocities), `F` (3 NN forces), `RS` (3 NN row sums), `VE` (GX GY GZ element von Mises envelope), `VN` (NN nodal von Mises envelope), `CN` (NN counts), `UD` (NN deformation envelope: the largest distance each node moves away from the center of mass motion), `UP` (3 NN: the deformed shape at the moment of the largest floor force, each displacement less the center of mass displacement), `HI` (7 NH history values), and `UE` and `FE` (24 scratch each). Growable i32 array `SOL` (GX GY GZ solid flags, element e at (k times GY plus j) times GX plus i).

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` does the same for an i32 growable array, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. On the web an array that holds entries cannot grow past the room it first reserved, so every array is emptied before it is sized again. `at1(n)` returns n, or 1 when n is below 1.

The exported function `dr_views()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, registers the arrays with `dr_view_f64`: 1 IN, 2 OUT, 3 KE, 4 DB, 5 M, 6 X, 7 U, 8 V, 9 VE, 10 VN, 11 UD, 12 HI, 13 UP; and with `dr_view_i32`: 20 SOL. It returns 0.

The exported function `dr_init()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, sizes IN to 64 zeros and OUT to 64 zeros with `zf`, calls `dr_views()` and returns 0.

The exported function `dr_size(nn, gx, gy, gz, nh)`, declared with the export keyword with five i32 parameters (each converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, sets NN, GX, GY, GZ and NH, then sizes with `zf` to zeros, each size raised to at least 1 with `at1`: KE to 576, DB to 144, M to nn, X, U, V, F, RS and UP to 3 nn, VE to gx times gy times gz, VN, CN and UD to nn, HI to 7 nh, UE and FE to 24; and with `zi` SOL to gx times gy times gz zeros. It calls `dr_views()` and returns 0.

Element helper. `enode(i, j, k, a)` returns the grid node of corner a (0 to 7) of element (i, j, k): with NX equal to GX plus 1 and NY equal to GY plus 1, the node at grid point (p, q, r) is (r times NY plus q) times NX plus p, and the corners in order are (i, j, k), (i plus 1, j, k), (i plus 1, j plus 1, k), (i, j plus 1, k), (i, j, k plus 1), (i plus 1, j, k plus 1), (i plus 1, j plus 1, k plus 1) and (i, j plus 1, k plus 1).

Force helper. `forces()` fills F with 0.0, then for each element (i, j, k) with i below GX, j below GY and k below GZ whose SOL entry at (k times GY plus j) times GX plus i is not 0: UE[3 a plus c] is U[3 times enode(i, j, k, a) plus c] for a below 8 and c below 3; FE[r] for r below 24 is the sum over c below 24 of KE[24 r plus c] times UE[c]; F[3 times enode(i, j, k, a) plus c] goes down by FE[3 a plus c]. Then the six center stresses s0 to s5 are the sums over c below 24 of DB[24 r plus c] times UE[c] for r from 0 to 5, the von Mises stress v is the square root of 0.5 times ((s0 minus s1) squared plus (s1 minus s2) squared plus (s2 minus s0) squared) plus 3.0 times (s3 squared plus s4 squared plus s5 squared), and VE at that element keeps the larger of its value and v. It returns the strain energy: 0.5 times the sum over c below 24 of UE[c] times FE[c], summed over the elements.

The exported function `dr_start()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, prepares the run. Inputs: IN[0], IN[1] and IN[2] the unit drop direction (dx, dy, dz), IN[3] the impact speed, IN[4] gravity along the drop direction, IN[5] the floor penalty stiffness kp, IN[6] the Courant factor (0.9 when not above 0.0), IN[7] the longest time to run, IN[8] the quiet time after contact ends that stops the run, and IN[9] the number of steps between history records. It sets T, STEP, NREC, DONE, QUIET and TOUCH to 0, STRIDE to IN[9] as int raised to at least 1, and fills U, VE, VN, UD, HI, RS and UP with 0.0. MT is the sum of M. V[3 n plus c] is IN[3] times the direction component c for each node n with M[n] above 0.0, and 0.0 for the others. PF is the largest dx times X[3 n] plus dy times X[3 n plus 1] plus dz times X[3 n plus 2] over nodes n with M[n] above 0.0. Row sums: for each solid element and each corner a below 8 and c below 3, RS[3 times enode(i, j, k, a) plus c] goes up by the sum over c2 below 24 of the absolute value of KE[24 times (3 a plus c) plus c2]. The largest squared frequency w2 is the largest of (RS[3 n plus c] plus kp) over M[n] over nodes n with M[n] above 0.0 and c below 3 (1.0 when there are none). DT is the Courant factor times 2.0 over the square root of w2. E0 is 0.5 times MT times IN[3] squared. OUT[0] becomes DT. Returns 0.

The exported function `dr_step(nmax)`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 parameter (converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, takes up to nmax time steps and returns DONE. It returns DONE at once when DONE is 1. With the drop direction (dx, dy, dz), kp IN[5], gravity IN[4], and loop counter s below nmax while DONE is 0, each step:

1. se is forces().
2. Floor: fc and pen start at 0.0, ce at 0.0. For each node n with M[n] above 0.0, p is dx times (X[3 n] plus U[3 n]) plus dy times (X[3 n plus 1] plus U[3 n plus 1]) plus dz times (X[3 n plus 2] plus U[3 n plus 2]) minus PF; when p is above 0.0, f is kp times p, F[3 n] goes down by f times dx, F[3 n plus 1] by f times dy, F[3 n plus 2] by f times dz, fc goes up by f, ce goes up by 0.5 times kp times p squared, and pen keeps the largest p.
3. Motion: for each node n with M[n] above 0.0 and c below 3, with gravity force M[n] times IN[4] times the direction component c added to F[3 n plus c], the acceleration is F[3 n plus c] over M[n]; V[3 n plus c] goes up by the acceleration times DT, and then U[3 n plus c] goes up by V[3 n plus c] times DT. ke is 0.5 times the sum over those of M[n] times V[3 n plus c] squared. The center of mass velocity along the drop direction vc is the sum over nodes of M[n] times (dx times V[3 n] plus dy times V[3 n plus 1] plus dz times V[3 n plus 2]), over MT (0.0 when MT is not above 0.0), and the center of mass displacement (ux, uy, uz) is the mass-weighted mean of U by component.
4. Deformation: for each node n with M[n] above 0.0, UD[n] keeps the larger of its value and the length of (U[3 n] minus ux, U[3 n plus 1] minus uy, U[3 n plus 2] minus uz).
5. T goes up by DT and STEP by 1. OUT[2] becomes T and OUT[1] STEP. When fc is above OUT[3], OUT[3] becomes fc, OUT[4] becomes T, and UP[3 n plus c] becomes U[3 n plus c] minus the center of mass displacement component c (ux, uy or uz) for every node n and c below 3. When fc is above 0.0: OUT[5] becomes T when TOUCH is 0.0, TOUCH becomes 1.0, OUT[6] becomes T and QUIET becomes 0.0; otherwise when TOUCH is 1.0 QUIET goes up by DT. OUT[14] keeps the largest pen. OUT[7] becomes vc. The energy error, the absolute value of (ke plus se plus ce minus E0 minus MT times IN[4] times the center of mass displacement along the drop direction) over E0 (0.0 when E0 is not above 0.0), is kept as the largest in OUT[12].
6. When STEP modulo STRIDE is 0 and NREC is below NH, the record at 7 NREC gets T, fc, vc, ke, se, ce and pen, and NREC goes up by 1. OUT[15] becomes NREC.
7. DONE becomes 1 when T is at least IN[7], or when TOUCH is 1.0 and QUIET is at least IN[8].

The exported function `dr_finish()`, declared with the export keyword with an i32 result, averages the element envelope to the nodes: VN and CN are filled with 0.0; for each solid element each of its 8 nodes gets the element's VE added to VN and 1.0 added to CN; VN[n] is divided by CN[n] wherever CN[n] is above 0.0. OUT[8] becomes the largest VE, OUT[9] the largest VN, OUT[10] the largest UD and OUT[11] MT. Returns 0.

The `main()` function does nothing.
